{% extends "tutorial_base.html.j2" %}

{% block tutorial_body %}
        <!-- Intro Card -->
        <div class="doc-intro-card">
          <h2>Cutting Chassis Brackets, Shock Tabs &amp; Base Plates Alongside Tubing</h2>
          <p>
            Roll cages, bumpers, and suspension links take flat brackets right next to your tube work. Drop 2D <code class="mono">.dxf</code> and <code class="mono">.svg</code> drawings onto the same table as your 3D STEP files so you only maintain one set of cut charts.
          </p>
          <p style="margin-top: 0.75rem;">
            Load single parts or full <code class="mono">.zip</code> fabrication kits. Multi-part SVGs split into individual pieces with their own quantity counters. DXF <code class="mono">TEXT</code>/<code class="mono">MTEXT</code> labels and open scribe lines map straight to your torch or scribe. Fix wrong-scale customer drawings with 2-point snapping, nest 30-piece kits onto <code class="mono">48 &times; 96 in</code> (<code class="mono">1250 &times; 2500 mm</code>) plate, and export native <code class="mono">G20</code> (inch) or <code class="mono">G21</code> (metric) G-code for your table.
          </p>
          <div class="doc-intro-grid">
            <div class="doc-intro-stat">
              <span class="label">Drawing Input</span>
              <span class="value">Multi-File DXF, SVG &amp; ZIP</span>
            </div>
            <div class="doc-intro-stat">
              <span class="label">Scale Correction</span>
              <span class="value">Magnetic 2-Point Snap + &times;25.4</span>
            </div>
            <div class="doc-intro-stat">
              <span class="label">Machine Dialect</span>
              <span class="value">Native G20 (in) &amp; G21 (mm)</span>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Built-In Quick Sample Callout -->
        <div class="doc-callout tip" style="margin-top: 1.5rem; margin-bottom: 2rem;">
          <div class="doc-callout-title">💡 Try It Right Now: Built-In Flat Sheet DXF Kit</div>
          <p>
            Want to test 2D plate cutting right now? Click <strong>Flat Sheet DXF Kit</strong> under <strong>Quick CAD Fixtures</strong> in the left sidebar (or open parts from <code class="mono">examples/dxf-svg/</code>). You get a 5-file plate kit with 3-level nested cutouts, 2D common-line edges, angled gussets, bend-relief slits, and scribe marks in one click.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- ==================================================================
             STEP 1
             ================================================================== -->
        <article class="doc-step-card" id="step-1">
          <div class="doc-step-header">
            <span class="doc-step-badge">Step 01</span>
            <h2 class="doc-step-title">Set Machine Units (<code class="mono">in / G20</code> or <code class="mono">mm / G21</code>) &amp; Drop Multiple <code class="mono">.DXF</code>, <code class="mono">.SVG</code>, or <code class="mono">.ZIP</code> Files</h2>
          </div>

          <div class="doc-step-body">
            <p>
              Check your machine units first. Click <span class="ui-chip">⚙ Machines</span> in the top-right header or check your active machine in the left panel. Controllers like Langmuir FireControl and ShopSabre WinCNC default to Imperial (<code class="mono">in / G20</code>, IPM, PSI). LinuxCNC, EdingCNC, and SimCNC tables typically run Metric (<code class="mono">mm / G21</code>, mm/min, bar). Switch any profile between Imperial and Metric in one click without rebuilding controller settings.
            </p>
            <p style="margin-top: 0.75rem;">
              Next, drop your 2D files into the upload zone:
            </p>
            <ul class="doc-bullet-list">
              <li>Drop multiple <code class="mono">.dxf</code> files, <code class="mono">.svg</code> cutouts, or a <code class="mono">.zip</code> kit at once.</li>
              <li>Multi-part SVGs (like a sheet of 8 shock tabs) split into separate parts (<code class="mono">shock_tabs_1</code>, <code class="mono">shock_tabs_2</code>, etc.) with their own quantity counters.</li>
              <li>DXF <code class="mono">TEXT</code>, <code class="mono">MTEXT</code>, and open scribe lines map straight to marking paths without manual layer cleanup.</li>
            </ul>
          </div>

          <figure class="doc-figure">
            <div class="doc-figure-bar">
              <span class="doc-figure-caption"><span class="dot"></span> Step 1: Setting Machine Units (in / G20 vs mm / G21) and dropping multi-file DXF, SVG, or ZIP parts into the workspace</span>
              <span class="doc-figure-zoom">🔍 Click image to enlarge</span>
            </div>
            <div class="doc-figure-img-wrap">
              <picture>
                <source srcset="assets/dxf-svg-1-upload.webp" type="image/webp" />
                <img src="assets/dxf-svg-1-upload.png" alt="Step 1: Multi-file DXF and SVG dropzone with Langmuir FireControl in / G20 machine units toggle" width="2880" height="1800" loading="lazy" />
              </picture>
            </div>
          </figure>
        </article>

        <!-- ==================================================================
             STEP 2
             ================================================================== -->
        <article class="doc-step-card" id="step-2">
          <div class="doc-step-header">
            <span class="doc-step-badge">Step 02</span>
            <h2 class="doc-step-title">Confirm Sheet Gauge or Plate Thickness to Unlock Cut Charts &amp; G-Code</h2>
          </div>

          <div class="doc-step-body">
            <p>
              Unlike 3D STEP models, flat DXF and SVG files carry no wall thickness. Running 16ga speeds on 1/2" plate wrecks nozzles, and running 1/2" pierce delays on 16ga blows holes in your sheet.
            </p>
            <p style="margin-top: 0.75rem;">
              You pick the plate thickness before G-code unlocks:
            </p>
            <ul class="doc-bullet-list">
              <li>The status badge shows <span class="ui-chip amber">Status: Awaiting Thickness</span>.</li>
              <li>Click your gauge pill: Imperial tables pick <span class="ui-chip">16ga</span>, <span class="ui-chip">14ga</span>, <span class="ui-chip">11ga</span>, <span class="ui-chip">1/8"</span>, <span class="ui-chip">3/16"</span>, <span class="ui-chip">1/4"</span>, <span class="ui-chip">3/8"</span>, or <span class="ui-chip">1/2"</span>; Metric tables pick <span class="ui-chip">1.5 mm</span>, <span class="ui-chip">3.0 mm</span>, <span class="ui-chip">6.0 mm</span>, etc.</li>
              <li>The badge turns green <span class="ui-chip emerald">Status: Ready</span> and loads matching amperage, torch height, pierce delay, and kerf width from your cut chart.</li>
            </ul>
          </div>

          <figure class="doc-figure">
            <div class="doc-figure-bar">
              <span class="doc-figure-caption"><span class="dot"></span> Step 2: Selecting 3/16" plate thickness to satisfy the safety gate and load matching Hypertherm cut chart parameters</span>
              <span class="doc-figure-zoom">🔍 Click image to enlarge</span>
            </div>
            <div class="doc-figure-img-wrap">
              <picture>
                <source srcset="assets/dxf-svg-2-thickness-gate.webp" type="image/webp" />
                <img src="assets/dxf-svg-2-thickness-gate.png" alt="Step 2: Sheet thickness selection pills from 16ga to 1/2 inch with Awaiting Thickness safety gate" width="2880" height="1800" loading="lazy" />
              </picture>
            </div>
          </figure>
        </article>

        <!-- ==================================================================
             STEP 3
             ================================================================== -->
        <article class="doc-step-card" id="step-3">
          <div class="doc-step-header">
            <span class="doc-step-badge">Step 03</span>
            <h2 class="doc-step-title">Rescale Mystery-Unit Drawings with Magnetic 2-Point Snapping (or <code class="mono">&times;25.4</code> / <code class="mono">&divide;25.4</code>)</h2>
          </div>

          <div class="doc-step-body">
            <p>
              Customer brackets and downloaded gussets often arrive at the wrong scale. A 4.5" tab saved in millimeters without a unit header imports 25.4 times too small (or 25.4 times too big the other way).
            </p>
            <p style="margin-top: 0.75rem;">
              Fix the size right on your preview without reopening CAD:
            </p>
            <ul class="doc-bullet-list">
              <li>Click <span class="ui-chip">Scale Part</span> above the part preview.</li>
              <li>Click any two known points: bolt centers, slot ends, tab widths, or corners. Your cursor snaps to exact CAD vertices (<code class="mono">P1 Snap</code> and <code class="mono">P2 Snap</code>).</li>
              <li>Check the measured span between both points (e.g., <code class="mono">Measured: 3.0000 in</code>).</li>
              <li>Enter your real dimension in <code class="mono">Target</code> (e.g., <code class="mono">4.5000 in</code>) and click <span class="ui-chip">Apply Scale</span>.</li>
              <li>For straight inch/mm mismatches, click <span class="ui-chip">&times;25.4</span> or <span class="ui-chip">&divide;25.4</span> for a 1-click fix.</li>
              <li><code class="mono">Apply to all parts in session</code> stays unchecked by default so scaling one supplier drawing never distorts the rest of your kit.</li>
            </ul>
          </div>

          <figure class="doc-figure">
            <div class="doc-figure-bar">
              <span class="doc-figure-caption"><span class="dot"></span> Step 3: Magnetic 2-point reference scaling with P1/P2 snapping, target dimension input, and unit flip buttons</span>
              <span class="doc-figure-zoom">🔍 Click image to enlarge</span>
            </div>
            <div class="doc-figure-img-wrap">
              <picture>
                <source srcset="assets/dxf-svg-3-scale-part.webp" type="image/webp" />
                <img src="assets/dxf-svg-3-scale-part.png" alt="Step 3: Magnetic 2-point Scale Part popover with P1/P2 snap, measured vs target dimension, and 25.4 flip buttons" width="2880" height="1800" loading="lazy" />
              </picture>
            </div>
          </figure>
        </article>

        <!-- ==================================================================
             STEP 4
             ================================================================== -->
        <article class="doc-step-card" id="step-4">
          <div class="doc-step-header">
            <span class="doc-step-badge">Step 04</span>
            <h2 class="doc-step-title">Nest Your Multi-Part Fabrication Kit onto a <code class="mono">96 &times; 48 in</code> (<code class="mono">2500 &times; 1250 mm</code>) Sheet</h2>
          </div>

          <div class="doc-step-body">
            <p>
              Once your parts are sized and paired with cut charts, open 2D Sheet Nesting to lay out your plate:
            </p>
            <ul class="doc-bullet-list">
              <li>Set part quantities for your kit (for example: 8 corner gussets, 16 shock tabs, and 6 winch plates, 30 pieces total).</li>
              <li>Enter your plate size (e.g. <code class="mono">96 &times; 48 in</code> or <code class="mono">2500 &times; 1250 mm</code>) and part gap (e.g. <code class="mono">0.250 in</code> / <code class="mono">6.35 mm</code>).</li>
              <li>Click <span class="ui-chip cyan">Nest Sheet</span> to pack all 30 parts cleanly inside your sheet margins.</li>
              <li><strong>Interactive Canvas Drag &amp; Halos:</strong> Drag parts in <code class="mono">&Delta;X, &Delta;Y</code>, rotate in <code class="mono">15&deg;</code> steps or free angles, and flip horizontally or vertically right on the sheet. Green and red halos warn you about spacing or edge collisions before you export G-code. Click <span class="ui-chip">Reset Auto-Nest</span> anytime to snap back (<code class="mono">Maker &le; 5 parts</code>, <code class="mono">Pro Shop unlimited</code>).</li>
              <li><strong>Cutout-in-Cutout Nesting:</strong> Small tabs and washers pack inside the open dropouts of larger brackets up to three levels deep. Innermost cutouts always cut before outer perimeters, so nested parts never tip into the water table before the torch cuts them.</li>
              <li><strong>2D Common-Line Cutting:</strong> Check <span class="ui-chip">Common-line cutting</span> to butt matching straight edges together with zero gap. One kerf-compensated pass cuts both parts, slashing pierces, cut time, and skeleton scrap.</li>
              <li>Interior bolt holes and slots always cut before the outer profile, keeping parts anchored to the sheet while holes pierce.</li>
              <li>Open slits and scribe marks stay kerf-free and follow their exact CAD centerlines.</li>
            </ul>
          </div>

          <figure class="doc-figure">
            <div class="doc-figure-bar">
              <span class="doc-figure-caption"><span class="dot"></span> Step 4: 2D XY Sheet Nesting of 30 gussets, shock tabs, and winch plates on a 96 &times; 48 in plate with 0.25 in spacing</span>
              <span class="doc-figure-zoom">🔍 Click image to enlarge</span>
            </div>
            <div class="doc-figure-img-wrap">
              <picture>
                <source srcset="assets/dxf-svg-4-sheet-nesting.webp" type="image/webp" />
                <img src="assets/dxf-svg-4-sheet-nesting.png" alt="Step 4: 2D XY sheet nesting of 30 parts on a 96 by 48 inch sheet with Imperial spacing and live G-code" width="2880" height="1800" loading="lazy" />
              </picture>
            </div>
          </figure>
        </article>

        <!-- ==================================================================
             STEP 5
             ================================================================== -->
        <article class="doc-step-card" id="step-5">
          <div class="doc-step-header">
            <span class="doc-step-badge">Step 05</span>
            <h2 class="doc-step-title">Verify Torch Motion in 3D with Live Imperial/Metric DRO &amp; Export Native <code class="mono">G20</code>/<code class="mono">G21</code> G-Code</h2>
          </div>

          <div class="doc-step-body">
            <p>
              Preview your torch path in the 3D simulator before striking an arc:
            </p>
            <ul class="doc-bullet-list">
              <li>Click <span class="ui-chip emerald">▶ Play</span> to watch torch travel across the nested plate.</li>
              <li>Track the live DRO for position (<code class="mono">X: 10.8969 in</code>, <code class="mono">Y: 27.1469 in</code>, <code class="mono">Z: 0.0600 in</code>), feedrate (<code class="mono">138 IPM</code>), and total travel (<code class="mono">Cut Dist: 3135.2 in</code>).</li>
              <li>Watch the amber <code class="mono">⚡ ARC ON</code> badge to make sure rapids lift to safe Z height clear of tip-ups.</li>
              <li>Follow the synced G-code viewer showing 4-decimal inch moves (<code class="mono">G1 X10.8969 Y27.1469 F138.0</code>) and a <code class="mono">G20</code> header for Langmuir FireControl or ShopSabre WinCNC. (For how <code class="mono">G94</code> and <code class="mono">G93</code> feedrates scale between inches and millimeters, see <a href="rotary-and-sheet-feedrates.html" style="color: var(--amber-400);">Tutorial 11: Understanding G94, G93 &amp; G20/G21 Feedrates</a>).</li>
              <li>Click <span class="ui-chip cyan">Download G-Code</span> to save your <code class="mono">.tap</code> or <code class="mono">.nc</code> file to USB or your machine PC.</li>
            </ul>
          </div>

          <figure class="doc-figure">
            <div class="doc-figure-bar">
              <span class="doc-figure-caption"><span class="dot"></span> Step 5: Simulating 3D torch travel with live Imperial DRO (138 IPM, 3135.2 in cut distance) and synchronized G20 G-code</span>
              <span class="doc-figure-zoom">🔍 Click image to enlarge</span>
            </div>
            <div class="doc-figure-img-wrap">
              <picture>
                <source srcset="assets/dxf-svg-5-imperial-sim.webp" type="image/webp" />
                <img src="assets/dxf-svg-5-imperial-sim.png" alt="Step 5: Live 3D cut simulation with Imperial DRO 138 IPM, Cut Dist 3135.2 in, and synced G20 G-code" width="2880" height="1800" loading="lazy" />
              </picture>
            </div>
          </figure>
        </article>

        <!-- ==================================================================
             FLAT-PLATE PRO CUTTING PRIMITIVES
             ================================================================== -->
        <article class="doc-step-card" id="flat-plate-primitives">
          <div class="doc-step-header">
            <span class="doc-step-badge" style="background: rgba(6, 182, 212, 0.15); color: var(--cyan-400); border-color: rgba(6, 182, 212, 0.35);">PRO TOOLS</span>
            <h2 class="doc-step-title">Heavy-Plate &amp; Nesting Tools: Open-Path Kerf, Edge-Start, Chain/Bridge, Corner Loops &amp; Void THC Lock</h2>
          </div>

          <div class="doc-step-body">
            <p>
              Use these five flat-plate tools when cutting heavy steel, open trim lines, tight nests, and clean remnants:
            </p>
            <ul class="doc-bullet-list">
              <li><strong>Open-Path Left / Right / Center Kerf (<code class="mono">Maker</code> &amp; <code class="mono">Pro Shop</code>):</strong> Pick <span class="ui-chip">Center (On-Line)</span>, <span class="ui-chip">Left</span>, or <span class="ui-chip">Right</span> kerf on open slit lines, edge trims, and bend reliefs. Offset side locks to the CAD stroke direction so trimmed plates hit exact size.</li>
              <li><strong>Thick-Plate Edge-Start Piercing (<code class="mono">Maker</code> &amp; <code class="mono">Pro Shop</code>):</strong> On plate <code class="mono">&ge; 12 mm</code> (<code class="mono">1/2&Prime;</code>), switch Lead-In Strategy to <span class="ui-chip">Edge-Start (Thick Plate)</span> (Edge Overhang defaults to <code class="mono">4.0 mm</code>). Your torch probes IHS on solid steel, lifts to pierce height, shifts off the plate edge to fire without slag blowback, and locks THC for the first <code class="mono">6.0 mm</code> back onto solid steel.</li>
              <li>
                <strong>Flared Chain &amp; Bridge Cutting (<code class="mono">Pro Shop</code>):</strong> Pick a chain mode inside Nesting's 2D sheet controls (Common-Line turns off while active):
                <ul class="doc-bullet-list" style="margin-top: 0.35rem; margin-bottom: 0.35rem;">
                  <li><strong><span class="ui-chip">Daisy-Chain (Single Pierce)</span>:</strong> Links nearby outer profiles into one continuous arc pass with flared bridges so you avoid retracing half-shapes.</li>
                  <li><strong><span class="ui-chip">Micro-Bridge (Connected)</span>:</strong> Leaves uncut holding tabs (Bridge Width auto-sizes from plate thickness or defaults to <code class="mono">3.0 mm</code>, up to <code class="mono">25.0 mm</code> link distance). Interior holes always cut first, and links across obstacles skip automatically.</li>
                </ul>
              </li>
              <li><strong>Void THC Auto-Lock:</strong> Every cut kerf slot and pierce crater on your sheet is tracked automatically. When a later cut or crop comes within <code class="mono">0.5 &times; kerf width + 4.0 mm</code> of a void, THC locks automatically so your torch never dives.</li>
              <li><strong>Flat Corner Loops:</strong> Turn on Corner Loops on flat sheet profiles to loop sharp outside corners without stalling torch speed. Tune Corner Loop Radius (default <code class="mono">3.0 mm</code>) per feature to match plate thickness and kerf.</li>
            </ul>
          </div>
        </article>

        <!-- ==================================================================
             TUTORIAL: REMNANT SKELETON GUILLOTINE CROP CUT
             ================================================================== -->
        <article class="doc-step-card" id="tutorial-remnant-crop">
          <div class="doc-step-header">
            <span class="doc-step-badge">Tutorial</span>
            <h2 class="doc-step-title">Tutorial: Cropping &amp; Saving a Clean Sheet Remnant (<code class="mono">Pro Shop</code>)</h2>
          </div>

          <div class="doc-step-body">
            <p>
              Instead of dragging jagged skeletons off your slats or torching freehand, slice a straight guillotine line behind your nested parts so unused steel drops off as a square, usable remnant.
            </p>
            <ul class="doc-bullet-list">
              <li><strong>Step 1. Nest Parts &amp; Open 2D Controls:</strong> Drop your <code class="mono">.dxf</code> or <code class="mono">.svg</code> parts, confirm sheet thickness, switch to <strong>Nesting</strong>, set quantities and sheet size, and open the 2D controls beside the 2D Pack Part Preview.</li>
              <li><strong>Step 2. Pick Crop Mode &amp; Clearance Margin:</strong> Open <span class="ui-chip">Guillotine Crop Cut</span> and choose <code class="mono">Best Single Cut (Max Remnant)</code>, <code class="mono">L-Cut (Both X &amp; Y)</code>, <code class="mono">Vertical (X)</code>, or <code class="mono">Horizontal (Y)</code>. Set <code class="mono">Margin</code> (default <code class="mono">5.0 mm</code> plus kerf, giving <code class="mono">kerf + 5.0 mm</code> clearance from the nearest part).</li>
              <li><strong>Step 3. Check the Crop Line &amp; Remnant Box:</strong> In the 2D Pack Part Preview SVG, check the dashed <code class="mono">CROP</code> line(s), off-sheet entry circle, and highlighted <code class="mono">Usable Remnant</code> dimensions before exporting G-code.</li>
              <li><strong>Step 4. Run the Cut Safely:</strong> Click <span class="ui-chip emerald">⚡ Compile Pack G-Code</span> and run it at the table. All interior holes and parts cut first. Then your torch starts off-sheet with automatic Void THC lock across kerf lines, dropping a clean rectangular remnant ready for your plate rack.</li>
            </ul>

            <div class="doc-callout tip" style="margin-top: 1.25rem;">
              <div class="doc-callout-title">✓ Tip: Best Single Cut vs. L-Cut (Both X &amp; Y)</div>
              <p style="margin-bottom: 0;">
                Pick <code class="mono">Best Single Cut (Max Remnant)</code> when parts span most of one sheet axis and one straight cut yields the biggest drop. Pick <code class="mono">L-Cut (Both X &amp; Y)</code> when parts cluster in the bottom-left corner to reclaim both a side strip and an end drop in two passes.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </article>

        <!-- ==================================================================
             ROTARY TUBE-WRAP & TEXT/MTEXT SCRIBING (v0.8-D)
             ================================================================== -->
        <article class="doc-step-card" id="rotary-tube-wrap">
          <div class="doc-step-header">
            <span class="doc-step-badge" style="background: rgba(6, 182, 212, 0.15); color: var(--cyan-400); border-color: rgba(6, 182, 212, 0.35);">APPLY DXF/SVG TO STEP TUBES</span>
            <h2 class="doc-step-title">Apply 2D DXF/SVG Cut Art &amp; <code class="mono">TEXT</code>/<code class="mono">MTEXT</code> Scribing onto Loaded STEP Tubes</h2>
          </div>

          <div class="doc-step-body">
            <p>
              Load a 3D STEP tube or assembly, click any Round Pipe or Square/Rectangular HSS solid, and open <strong>DXF/SVG Tube Overlay</strong> right under the <strong>3D Part Preview</strong> (<code class="mono">Maker</code> &amp; <code class="mono">Pro Shop</code>). Wrap logos, slots, and part labels onto your 3D tube live without unfolding in CAD.
            </p>
            <ul class="doc-bullet-list">
              <li><strong>Position &amp; Clocking (Axial Offset X &amp; Clock Angle A):</strong> Keep <span class="ui-chip">Center along tube X</span> checked to center artwork along the tube, or uncheck it to enter <span class="ui-chip">Axial Offset X</span>. Dial <span class="ui-chip">Rotation Angle A&deg;</span> to clock the art anywhere around the tube.</li>
              <li><strong>Scale Mode &amp; Axis Swap:</strong> Pick <span class="ui-chip">1:1 Literal Dimensions</span> to hold exact drawing inches or millimeters on the tube, or <span class="ui-chip">Stretch to Circumference</span> to wrap full-width art around the perimeter. Check <span class="ui-chip">Swap X / Y Axes</span> if your 2D file was drawn sideways.</li>
              <li><strong>Cut vs. Scribe / Plasma Mark &amp; DXF <code class="mono">TEXT</code>/<code class="mono">MTEXT</code>:</strong> Choose <span class="ui-chip">Auto (Cut Contours + Scribe Layers)</span>, <span class="ui-chip">Cut All Through</span>, <span class="ui-chip">Scribe All (Mark)</span>, or <span class="ui-chip">Plasma Mark All</span>. DXF <code class="mono">TEXT</code> and <code class="mono">MTEXT</code> convert straight into single-stroke markings wrapped with your cuts.</li>
            </ul>
          </div>
        </article>
{% endblock %}
